# Project Rules — improve-my-sound

## What we build
"improve-my-sound" (brand: aintonio.dev | Antonio Gómez) is a voice/podcast
post-production tool powered by a neural network. Hero: pass your (rough/noisy)
voice audio in, get professionally-enhanced audio out. Target users: content
creators and podcasters; the brand owner is an AI Engineer running a 30-day
content challenge (the first video launches this product).

## Architecture (decided)
- **Core**: a Python package + CLI named `voxera` (e.g. `voxera enhance <input> -o <output>`).
  The core is the source of truth and the primary interface.
- **Backends are pluggable**: `--backend {deepfilternet|dpdfnet|resemble}`. The
  neural-network engine is swappable; model/param selection is driven by measured
  Pareto quality-vs-RTF (see Metrics). Whichever backend wins plugs into the same
  `enhance()` contract — do NOT hard-couple to one model.
- **Desktop (later, out of scope for first features)**: a Tauri (Rust) app wrapping
  the CLI as a sidecar. Keep the core decoupled so Tauri can call it as a subprocess.
- **Premise (non-negotiable)**: everything must be drivable from the terminal so the
  AI agent can edit, run and tweak it all. No functionality that exists only behind
  a GUI. The CLI is the hero interface.

## Languages & layout
- Python for the core (`src/voxera/`, console entry `voxera`). Rust only later
  for the Tauri shell.
- Unit tests: pytest. Acceptance specs: Gherkin `.feature` files via the APS pipeline.

## Metrics (how we choose backends/params)
- Quality (no-reference, computable on real voice): **UTMOS** and **DNSMOS** (SIG/OVRL).
- Cost: **RTF** (real-time factor) and CPU vs GPU footprint.
- Evaluated on a public test set with **Spanish/Castilian + English** clips.
- Selection target: Pareto front of quality vs RTF (model choice is empirical, not assumed).

## Handoffs
- Terse, explicit; report state + request role-appropriate review. No process narrative.

## Ownership
- Do not edit another role's prompt or workflow ownership without explicit user direction.

## Current work
- **Feature 1 (in progress)**: happy-path end-to-end CLI — `voxera enhance <input.wav>
  -o <output.wav>` loads an audio file, runs ONE pluggable backend's enhancement, and
  writes the improved file. Define the backend-agnostic `enhance()` contract, then wire
  ONE concrete backend (default `dpdfnet`) to prove the path end-to-end. Error/edge
  cases (bad path, unsupported format, empty audio) are explicit scenarios.
